WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.749 --> 00:00:04.430
For every horror movie that gets a mainstream
audience, there are a dozen that fly under

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.040
the radar on their release.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.140
So where can you find all the great horror
movies you've never heard of?

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:10.840
One word: Netflix.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:14.809
These underrated horror gems never got the
attention they deserved, but now you can revel

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:18.990
in all the terror they have to offer from
the comfort of your living room.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:19.990
Holidays

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:24.670
Anthology horror has been making a comeback,
and this dark riff on seasonal celebrations

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:26.710
is one of the lesser-known gems in the genre.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:31.330
Eight different directors, including Kevin
Smith and Gary Shore, contributed a creepy

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:34.140
pint-sized nightmare on a holiday theme.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.790
Like most anthology films, not every one of
these scary stories will hit home, but that's

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.290
also part of the charm.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:44.360
The wildly inventive story lines and diverse
approaches showcased in each of its short

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.770
films make Holidays the kind of movie that
has something for everyone.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:49.770
Hard Candy

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:54.480
Long before she was a well-known name in Hollywood,
Ellen Page was the young star of this immensely

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.960
disturbing movie about a 14-year-old girl
who's targeted by a middle-aged predator,

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.890
played by Patrick Wilson—only for the predator
to realize he's the hunted, not the hunter.

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:08.470
Page carries the movie to a crazy conclusion
that left no question about her star potential

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:09.470
at the time.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:13.840
The controversial premise and minuscule budget
made Hard Candy a tough sell when it came

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.170
to a traditional release.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:20.290
But despite being awarded Overlooked Film
of the Year at the 2006 Phoenix Film Critics

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:25.470
Society Awards, the movie has continued to
be a hidden and little-seen gem.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:26.950
Curve

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.619
In this horror flick about a road trip gone
wrong, Julianne Hough stars as Mallory, a

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:35.780
bride-to-be whose car breaks down in the wild
en route to her wedding rehearsal.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:40.470
When a charming but sadistic hitchhiker enters
the picture, Mallory's last hurrah as a single

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:44.880
girl turns into a fight for survival: she
crashes her car and ends up trapped at the

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.590
bottom of a gulch, while the hitchhiker who
survived the crash returns periodically to

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:50.590
taunt her.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:54.451
Curve should've made a bigger splash than
it did, but it got lost in early 2016 and

00:01:54.451 --> 00:01:55.860
went straight to streaming.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.000
However, the theater's loss was Netflix's
gain, so catch it on the streaming service

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.630
before it disappears 'round the bend.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.659
The Lazarus Effect

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:09.270
Released in 2015, this movie about a serum
that can bring people back to life was almost

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:10.509
completely overlooked.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:16.310
On sheer star power alone, The Lazarus Effect
is virtually unmatched in an under-the-radar

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:22.739
horror movie, with Olivia Wilde, Mark Duplass,
Donald Glover, Sarah Bolger, and Evan Peters

00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:29.110
all working their magic to elevate a less-than-inspired
concept into legitimately scream-worthy territory.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:32.660
And while it's true that the script could
be tighter, don't believe the lack of hype

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:37.540
on this one: The Lazarus Effect is a far better
movie than its low rating on Rotten Tomatoes

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.430
would suggest.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.050
1922

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:44.519
Netflix definitely knew what it was doing
when it snagged this little story for a screen

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:46.010
adaptation.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:51.260
Based on a novella from Stephen King's Full
Dark, No Stars anthology, 1922 stars Thomas

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:56.720
Jane as Wilfred James, a farmer living in
rural Nebraska during the period leading up

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.120
to the Great Depression.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.230
Money troubles and marital conflict lead Wilfred
to some very dark places—and it just goes

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:08.480
to show that sometimes, getting away with
a crime is much worse than getting caught.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.599
"I'd axed my wife to keep my home.

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:18.099
The only way I'd be leaving it now would be
in chains."

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:23.239
Like many of Stephen King's best works, this
is a character-driven horror, and strong performances

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:28.690
make it a standout—particularly from Jane,
who immerses himself in the role so thoroughly

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:33.810
that it's hard to believe this same miserable,
hay-chewing yokel is the same cosmopolitan

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.910
pretty boy from The Sweetest Thing.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:38.560
"You're never gonna find anyone better than
my brother here.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:41.030
Here is the greatest and I am sure.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.940
Just look at him, chicks freak on this guy."

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:44.940
Creep

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:50.830
In 2014, filmmaker Patrick Brice set out to
answer the question "What's the worst thing

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.780
that could happen from meeting someone on
Craigslist?"

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:58.720
Without spoiling anything, let's just say
that the answer is something really bad.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.269
That's what a videographer discovers when
he goes to the home of Josef—played by Mark

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:08.250
Duplass, who also co-wrote and produced the
movie—for what's supposed to be a documentary

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.500
project.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:14.050
What starts out as the ordinary weirdness
of internet strangers becomes something much

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:15.270
more disturbing.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.310
"Oh that's Peach Fuzz.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:19.190
That's just Peach Fuzz.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:20.859
Say hello."

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:24.870
The result is a movie that more than lives
up to its title.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.080
Gerald's Game

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:31.590
With so many blockbusters the big screen in
2017, you might have missed some of the smaller

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:37.280
adaptations of Stephen King's works—including
this one based on the 1992 novel of the same

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:38.280
name.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:42.150
Gerald's Game centers on a woman named who
ends up trapped, handcuffed to the bed at

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:45.070
her lake house after her husband has a heart
attack.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:49.710
As she struggles to find a way to escape,
she's visited by a series of entities—some

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:55.032
real, some imagined, and some that seem to
straddle the line between this world and the

00:04:55.032 --> 00:04:56.560
next.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.080
"Who are you?"

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:06.620
One of Netflix's original feature films, Gerald's
Game was eagerly anticipated by hardcore King

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:11.120
fans but didn't get the kind of widespread
attention merited by its creativity and star

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.150
power.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:17.310
Despite the obvious challenges of holding
down an entire two-hour film on her own, actress

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:22.940
Carla Gugino keeps it riveting, making this
the finest horror movie set almost entirely

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:26.020
on a bed since, well, maybe ever.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.340
Unless you count Bed of the Dead.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.340
A Dark Song

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:35.140
This Irish independent horror movie flew under
the radar when it released in 2017, but the

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:39.130
handful of film buffs who did see it were
impressed—and with good reason.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:44.030
A Dark Song centers on a grieving mother who
hires an occultist to guide her through a

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:48.210
complex, months-long rite at a remote home
in the Welsh countryside.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:53.270
Despite a teensy budget and a very green director
at its helm, A Dark Song boasts incredible

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:58.600
production values and phenomenal sound work
that subverts the usual tried-and-true horror

00:05:58.600 --> 00:05:59.730
film tropes.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:05.270
Instead, the film revels in the slow burn
and deliberately draws out the tension.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:11.150
In the end, this entry is a deep, dark, and
gorgeously acted meditation on loss that builds

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:13.870
into a harrowing and unexpected climax.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:15.370
Thanks for watching!

